---
id: neighbor-policies
title: BGP neighbor routing policies
summary: Apply import and export policies to individual BGP neighbors.
headline: Filter routes and choose an exit

instructions: |
  Both edges advertise the server LAN. r1 imports edge2 routes with local
  preference 200, so client1's ping uses edge2 despite its higher MED.
  edge1's export policy sets MED and a community only on the server prefix.
  Other routes are permitted by the final catch-all clause.

  Experiments: edit the policy, then Reset/Launch.
  1. Change prefer-server's local preference to 50: traffic uses edge1.
  2. Change its first clause to action: deny: r1 uses edge1 for the server LAN.
  3. Remove the final permit clause to see implicit deny filter other prefixes.
  Inspect BGP routes to see the communities, MED, and local preference.

topology:
  name: neighbor-policies
  device: frr
  module: [bgp]
  plugin: [bgp.policy]
  bgp:
    sessions:
      ipv4: [ebgp]
      ipv6: []

  routing:
    prefix:
      server: {ipv4: &server-prefix 203.0.113.0/24}
    policy:
      prefer-server:
        - match: {prefix: server}
          set: {locpref: 200}
        - action: permit
      tag-server:
        - match: {prefix: server}
          set:
            med: 10
            community: {standard: ['65002:7']}
        - action: permit

  nodes:
    lan:
      device: switch
      role: switch
    r1:
      bgp:
        as: 65001
        locpref: 100
    edge1:
      bgp:
        as: &edge-as 65002
        advertise: [*server-prefix]
      routing:
        static:
          - ipv4: client1.prefix
            nexthop: edge2.ipv4.lan
            distance: 250
    edge2:
      bgp:
        as: *edge-as
        advertise: [*server-prefix]
    client1:
      device: linux
      role: host
      validation:
        actions:
          - label: Follow ICMP to client2
            type: ping
            target: client2
            count: 5
    client2:
      device: linux
      role: host
      routing:
        static:
          - ipv4: 0.0.0.0/0
            nexthop: edge1.ipv4.lan
      validation:
        actions:
          - label: Ping back to client1
            type: ping
            target: client1
            count: 3

  links:
    - interfaces:
        - node: r1
        - node: edge1
          bgp:
            policy: {out: tag-server}
    - interfaces:
        - node: r1
          bgp:
            policy: {in: prefer-server}
        - node: edge2
          bgp:
            med: 50
    - prefix:
        ipv4: 192.0.2.0/24
      interfaces:
        - node: r1
          ipv4: 192.0.2.1/24
        - node: client1
          ipv4: 192.0.2.10/24
    - prefix:
        ipv4: *server-prefix
      interfaces:
        - node: edge1
          ipv4: 203.0.113.1/24
        - node: lan
          ipv4: false
    - prefix:
        ipv4: *server-prefix
      interfaces:
        - node: edge2
          ipv4: 203.0.113.2/24
        - node: lan
          ipv4: false
    - prefix:
        ipv4: *server-prefix
      interfaces:
        - node: client2
          ipv4: 203.0.113.10/24
        - node: lan
          ipv4: false

  validate:
    - label: Both eBGP paths are available
      state:
        - node: r1
          bgp:
            - peer: edge1
              asn: edge1.asn
              state: established
            - peer: edge2
              asn: edge2.asn
              state: established
    - label: Import policy selects edge2 with higher local preference
      state:
        - node: r1
          routes:
            - prefix: client2.prefix
              protocol: bgp
              nexthop: edge2.ipv4.r1
    - label: Both edges have a return route to client1
      state:
        - node: edge1
          routes:
            - prefix: client1.prefix
              protocol: bgp
              nexthop: r1.ipv4.edge1
        - node: edge2
          routes:
            - prefix: client1.prefix
              protocol: bgp
              nexthop: r1.ipv4.edge2
    - label: ICMP reaches client2 and returns
      packet:
        from: client1
        type: icmp-echo
        target: client2.ipv4
        expect:
          type: icmp-echo-reply
          from: client2
---
